The processed data from the upstream layers provides the foundation for Dimension and Fact processing. Table-specific business transformations are then applied to generate the required analytical datasets.
10.3 Dimension Tables
The project contains 15 Dimension tables implemented in Microsoft Fabric.
Dimension tables contain descriptive and reference information required for reporting and analytical processing. They provide the business context associated with the transactional or measurable information maintained in the Fact tables.
Each Dimension table is created using its own transformation code based on the corresponding SQL implementation.
Depending on the business requirement, Dimension processing may include:
- Reading the required source datasets
- Joining related source tables
- Applying required filters
- Selecting and mapping columns
- Applying business rules
- Creating derived attributes
- Performing data type conversions
- Handling null or default values
- Removing duplicates where required
- Preparing required keys and reference values
- Loading the final Dimension table into Fabric
The exact transformation logic differs for each Dimension table depending on its source data and business purpose.
10.4 Fact Tables
The project contains 3 Fact tables implemented in Microsoft Fabric.
Fact tables contain transactional, event-level, or measurable business information required for reporting and analytics.
Each Fact table has its own transformation logic based on the existing SQL implementation and business requirements.
Depending on the table, Fact processing may include:
- Reading the required source datasets
- Joining multiple source tables
- Referencing related Dimension tables where required
- Applying business filters and conditions
- Mapping required keys
- Performing calculations
- Creating derived measures and attributes
- Applying date and timestamp transformations
- Handling null or default values
- Maintaining the required Fact table grain
- Selecting the required target columns
- Loading the final Fact table into Fabric
The Fact tables provide the measurable business information required for downstream reporting and analytical consumption.
10.5 Transformation and Loading Process
Although each Dimension and Fact table has different business logic, the overall transformation and loading approach follows a common process.
Source Data Selection
The required source datasets are identified based on the existing SQL implementation and target table requirements.
The appropriate processed data is read from the Fabric Lakehouse, and only the required columns are selected for further processing.
Data Transformation
After the source data is selected, table-specific business transformations are applied.
The transformation may include joins, filters, column mappings, conditional logic, derived columns, calculations, data type conversions, null handling, duplicate handling, key mapping, and date transformations.
The Fabric transformation logic is developed to maintain the required business outcome of the corresponding SQL implementation.
Dimension Transformation
For Dimension tables, the transformation focuses mainly on preparing descriptive and reference information.
Required business attributes are selected and transformed into the target Dimension structure. Keys and reference values are prepared where required to support relationships with the Fact tables.
Fact Transformation
For Fact tables, the transformation focuses on preparing transactional and measurable information.
Required source datasets are combined according to the defined business relationships. Dimension information is incorporated where required, and the necessary calculations, measures, and target attributes are generated.
The required Fact grain is maintained throughout processing so that each record represents the intended business-level information.
Target Mapping and Loading
After transformation, the resulting dataset is aligned with the target Fabric table structure.
Column names, data types, keys, required attributes, and mandatory values are validated before the final load.
The transformed dataset is then loaded into the corresponding Dimension or Fact table in Microsoft Fabric.
The overall transformation process is:
